::instructions

If using already prepared (tomato sauce), skip the tomato preparation below.
Otherwise:
[
    (San Marzano plum tomatoes, fresh) <wash>
        <trim, green stems>
        <cut, into a few chunks>
    Lightly salt the chunks over a (colander) or (strainer) to release water and help them break down faster.
]
-OR-
[
    (San Marzano plum tomatoes, canned peeled) <crush, by hand>
    Do not use (food processor) for the tomatoes.
]
If preparing sauce from tomatoes, cook them down in (stock pot, for tomato sauce) for approximately 30 minutes. Then remove skins and seeds using (food mill) or (strainer) to obtain {tomato sauce}.
{meatball mixture} = (ground meat) + (Pecorino Romano cheese) + (breadcrumbs) + (egg) + (milk) + (parsley) + (basil, for meatballs) + (onion, for meatballs) + (garlic, for meatballs)
    <mix, by hand>
    <form, equal-sized meatballs>
[
    {meatballs} <bake, in (oven), 400°F> ?= brown
]
-OR-
[
    Reserve {meatballs} to fry with the other meats in the next step.
]
(sauce pot) <coat, bottom, with (olive oil)>
Sear (pork tenderloin), (Italian sausage) and (stewing beef) one by one in (sauce pot), removing each when browned outside. Fry the reserved meatballs here if using that method. Do not cook the meats all the way through.
(white onion) + (garlic, for sauce)
    <sweat, in (sauce pot)>
(sauce pot) <deglaze, with (red wine)>
Return the browned meats and meatballs to (sauce pot), with the prepared tomato sauce and (basil leaves).
{ragù} <season, with (dry oregano) + (red pepper) + (salt) + (pepper) + (sugar), to taste>
    <cook, very low flame, uncovered, 4-5h>
Meanwhile [
    {ragù} <stir, ensuring it does not burn>
]
{ragù} <serve, over (pasta)>

::notes

The tomato sauce can be made beforehand and frozen or canned for months, as described by the source.
Alternatively freeze the finished sauce and meats in containers for up to 4-6 months.
The source lists optional tomato paste but gives no addition step.
Pasta guidance linked by the source: /pasta.

::story

This recipe is known by Italian-Americans as "Sunday Gravy", which originated from the Southern Italian dish Ragu Napoletano. This is my variation of my family's version which was passed down 3 generations to me.


::source
<<<
---
title: "Ragu Napoletano"
date: 2021-05-13
tags: ['sauce', 'italian']
author: mfed3
---

This recipe is known by Italian-Americans as "Sunday Gravy", which originated from the Southern Italian dish Ragu Napoletano. This is my variation of my family's version which was passed down 3 generations to me.

![Ragu Napoletano](/pix/ragu-napoletano.webp)

- ⏲️ Prep time: 30-60 min
- 🍳Cook time: 4-5 hrs
- 🍽️ Servings: 10-12

## Ingredients

### Tomato Sauce
- 6-10 lbs San Marzano plum tomatoes (fresh or canned peeled)
- Tomato paste (optional)

### Aromatics and Herbs
- 8 cloves garlic
- 1.5 white onions
- 6-8 oz red wine
- 8 fresh basil leaves
- Dry oregano
- Red pepper

### Meat
- 1-2 lb pork tenderloin
- 1-2 lb sweet and or spicy Italian sausage
- 1 lb cubed stewing beef (chuck, short rib, or oxtail)

### Meatballs
- 1-2 lb total of ground beef chuck, veal, and pork
- 1 1/2 cups grated Pecorino Romano cheese
- 1 1/2 cups breadcrumbs (baked stale bread, oregano, salt and pepper pulsed in a food processor)
- 2-3 eggs
- Splash of milk
- Handfull of fresh parsley chopped
- Sprig of fresh basil chopped
- 1/2 onion minced
- 4-8 cloves garlic minced

## Directions

1. If you already have tomato sauce skip to step 5
2. For fresh tomatoes, wash and cut out off the green stems of the tomatoes and cut them into a few chunks and lightly salt them over a collander or strainer so they release water and break down quicker.
3. For canned tomatoes, use your hands to crush the peeled tomatoes (don't use a food processor).
4. In a large stock pot cook down the tomatoes for approximately 30 mins then use a food mill or strainer to remove the skins and seeds to yield tomato sauce (this can be done beforehand and frozen / canned for months).
5. Mix all the ingredients in the Meatballs section by hand and form equal sized meatballs.
6. Either bake the meatballs at 400°F until brown or fry them with the the other meats in step 7.
7. Coat the bottom of a dutch oven or large stock pot with olive oil and  sear each meat one by one, removing from the pot once browned on the outside (do not cook the meat all the way through).
8. Sweat the onion and garlic, then deglaze the pan with the red wine.
9. Fill the pot with the seared meat, tomato sauce, add the fresh basil, then season with dried oregano, red pepper, salt, pepper, and a pinch of sugar to taste.
10. Cook on very low flame uncovered for 4-5 hours stirring making sure it doesn't burn.
11. Serve over cooked dry [pasta](/pasta) such as ziti, penne, rigatoni.
    Alternatively, freeze the sauce and meats in containers for up to 4-6 months.
>>>
